Z||000001||stimpy||04-06-2001||02:48 PM||||What you say? That's just not right.<P>I gotta say though that any cast iron bell that can break when you hit it is the result of some shoddy workmanship. I understand the whole symbol of freedom thing, in todays disposable society, it would be melted into Hyundai's. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfixit58||04-06-2001||03:20 PM||||Stimpy,<P>I understand your point but there's a little more to the Liberty Bell and its manufacture then "shoddy workmanship". Way back in the day... before T.V. and radio, the towns people depended on their town bell to inform them of emergency meetings and other trouble. <P>During the War of Independence, the British (who originally cast the bell) were notorious for taking down the captured town's bell and melting it down to make cannons to use against the Colonists. This act both denied the town of a way to warn all their people in ear-shot of trouble (i.e., the British are coming) and demoralized them in their fight for independence. <P>The Liberty Bell survived only because, at great risk of their life, the towns people took it down and hid it during the war. This act can be compared to todays towns people hiding short wave radios to communicate with the other towns if we were invaded by a hostile foreign nation.<P>It's an interesting story. I'm not offended by your comment but just thought you'd like to know,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000003||stsmithgt||04-06-2001||03:54 PM||||That bell it tough.  It was made to be hit.<BR>The crack probably isn't shoddy workmanship.  Some "Homer Simson ancestor" could have slipped while taking it out of the bellfrie and it fell and hit the ground,"DOH!, look out below! Bonnngggg! CRACK!" <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> (I'm not too up on history, but the imagination fills it in pretty well.)||||reg||1
Z||000004||stimpy||04-06-2001||06:29 PM||||Mrfixit,<BR>I'm glad you could discern the satire in my comments. I think someone vandalizing the Liberty Bell would be right up there with painting graffitti on the Licoln Memorial for heinous acts of desecration.  I have yet to hear the story of what happened, but I truly hope they caught the perpetrator and will make him pay greatly. <P>As far as the original crack in the Liberty Bell, if my recollection of jr. high U.S. history is correct, that happened only the second time the bell was rung. Can't recall the occaision though. Any other history trivia folks have a clue?||||reg||1
