Z||000008||theogre||04-06-2001||08:39 PM||||I'll ring your bell Cliff. I have a nice 8# hammer for you. You wombat.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>Stimpy, I don't recall the bell being Iron. I think it's a poor grade brass.<P>What most people think it the crack isn't. It's an atempt to repair the bell. The actual crack starts above the wide, visible, repair, travels up the the raised bands near the top and then to the right. The crack spreads a little more each year.<P>Photo's of the attack by this ingrate show significant damage but far less than all the jacka$ses that broke peices off the bell for souveniers as it toured the country years ago. The Bell lost the whole bottom edge to that.<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000009||RNGCRSH||04-06-2001||10:31 PM||||The crack in the liberty bell is a result of cooling the bell to quickly after it was removed from the mold.<P>usualy when something so large is cast, it must be put into a low temperature fire and allowed to cool as the fire dies out and the embers glow no longer. <P>Just a tidbit of trivia.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Tigger||04-06-2001||11:52 PM||||Well, I know if you cool metal too fast, the structure matrix can build up very large internal stresses.  Give it a good *bong* and if the vibrations hit the natural frequency of the metal in that state, you'll get a crack.  I'd assume the crack is getting larger due to gravitational forces but primarily due to the remaining internal stresses that still exist.<P>As far as the guy hitting it with a hammer, what an idiot...||||reg||3
